<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cangas de Onís</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cangas de Onís is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, at the foot of the Picos de Europa. Tradition makes it the first seat of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a>, founded by Pelagius after his victory over a Muslim force at nearby Covadonga around 722, and the kings resided there until the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravia">Pravia</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>. Its chapel of Santa Cruz stands on the site of one built by Pelagius's son Favila.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cangas-de-onis.jpg" alt="Cangas de Onís" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbythyme · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_bridge_at_Cangas_1_com.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
